-- title:
--   Gauss normalisation of q-expansions with poles among those of j
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and work inside the field $L = \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ with its subfield `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subextension of $L$ generated by the coefficientwise image, under the map induced by $\mathbb Q \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$, of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` (the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb Q)$ obtained by adjoining the divisor expansions of level $N$ to $\mathbb Q$). Let $f$ be a non-zero element of this field, and assume: for every place $W$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar N` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ — that is, every proper valuation subring containing the image of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and which is a principal ideal ring — such that $W.\mathrm{ord}\, f < 0$, one also has $W.\mathrm{ord}$ of the element `jq` $=\mathfrak q^{-1}\cdot j_{\mathrm{num}}(\mathfrak q)$, viewed in `modularFunctionFieldBar N` through the same coefficientwise embedding, strictly negative; here $\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of the adic valuation attached to $W$. Let $A$ be an arbitrary valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. The conclusion is that there exists $c \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$, $c \ne 0$, such that every Laurent coefficient $a_k(c f)$, $k \in \mathbb Z$, lies in $A$, and for at least one index $k$ the coefficient $a_k(c f)$ is not a non-unit of $A$, i.e. is a unit of $A$.
--
--   This is the normalisation of the $q$-expansion of a modular function whose poles occur only at poles of $j$ so that its coefficients have Gauss norm $1$ with respect to a given valuation of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; it is the variant of the corresponding statement for functions with poles confined to the cusp $\bar\infty$. It is used in the construction of prolongations of places in the level-one specialisation argument, where integrality of the coefficients of a scaled $q$-expansion is required.
